  "prose": {
    "bottom_line": "Investment and crypto scams now drive 54% of reported fraud losses ($151M in six months, +26% year-over-year), with median victim loss climbing to $32K. Recovery pitches surged +336% in losses, re-victimizing the same cohort.",
    "key_judgements": [
      "Investment and crypto scams accounted for $151M in losses over six months, 54% of all reported fraud losses, with median loss per victim rising from $25K to $32K.",
      "Recovery pitch losses jumped +336% year-over-year to $16M, with median loss climbing from $7,550 to $17,575\u2014suggesting scammers are systematically re-targeting prior victims.",
      "Seniors lost 52% of investment scam dollars and 55% of recovery pitch losses, indicating concentrated exposure in this demographic.",
      "Text message contact share rose 2.2 percentage points while direct calls fell 1.0 point, suggesting fraudsters are shifting to asynchronous channels that evade real-time intervention."
    ],
    "watch_list": [
      "Deepfakes and AI voice cloning appeared in 6 recent bulletins and could amplify bank investigator impersonation (899 reports, $12M) and emergency scams by making spoofed authority figures more convincing.",
      "One-time passcode theft tactics featured in 4 bulletins and directly enable phishing-to-account-takeover (2,517 reports) and bank impersonation schemes.",
      "Fake payment notifications via email or text (1 bulletin) could expand seller-side overpayment fraud, which saw +40% loss growth despite fewer reports."
    ],
    "recommendation_rationales": [
      "Investment and crypto scams generated $151M in six months with robust +26% loss growth and median victim loss at $32K; flagging repeated escalating transfers to crypto on-ramps targets the payment method cited in 15 bulletins and addresses the typology's medium bank-transfer exposure before losses crystallize.",
      "Recovery pitch losses exploded +336% to $16M with median loss more than doubling to $17,575, and 55% of losses hit seniors; proactive outreach to known victims is low-effort and directly interrupts re-victimization at first contact, when intervention is cheapest.",
      "Job scams recorded $16M in losses with high bank-transfer exposure (cited in 3 bulletins) and 11% senior loss share, suggesting younger victims; campus awareness campaigns are low-cost and target the demographic most likely to encounter fake job offers before they become money mules."
    ]
  }
}